&lt;p&gt;Columbia Chinese Students Club is so excited to host its annual Night Market again!&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We&amp;rsquo;re bringing a night market like you would see in the streets of Beijing to Columbia! Come experience Chinese culture on Low Plaza, complete with food from restaurants around New York City and amazing performances from Columbia&amp;rsquo;s talented student performance groups. The market will open at 6 pm, with performances starting around 7 pm and concluding around 10 pm. We can&amp;rsquo;t wait to see you there!&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This event is open to CUID holders only. General&amp;nbsp;tickets are free, and food tickets are available to pre-purchase for use during the Night Market. Food tickets do not have monetary value, and are not refundable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As October begins, we honor the strength, courage, and resilience of everyone affected by breast cancer. In observance of &lt;strong&gt;Breast Cancer Awareness&lt;/strong&gt; Month, Columbia University&amp;rsquo;s Fall 2026 Blood Drive Campaign is dedicated to raising awareness of this disease and providing vital support to cancer patients and everyone who relies on donated blood. According to the World Health Organization, breast cancer accounts for about 30% of all new cancer cases in the United States.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Every pint donated is more than just a unit of blood &amp;ndash; it&amp;rsquo;s hope, strength, and another chance at life for someone fighting their hardest battle.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The data center roll out to power AI is having immediate and long term health and environmental impacts&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The data center roll out to power AI is having immediate and long term health and environmental impacts. To report on these impacts journalists need to understand how air, noise and water pollution impacts human health, the way those pollutants are measured and how corporations are exploiting the permitting process to get their projects approved.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.eventbrite.com/e/reporting-on-the-health-and-environmental-impacts-of-data-centers-tickets-2002479060776?aff=oddtdtcreator&amp;amp;keep_tld=true" target="_blank"&gt;More information and registration page&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Registration REQUIRED by 4pm on October 16, 2026 in order to attend this event.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Please join the Harriman Institute and the Department of Slavic Languages for a joint book launch celebrating three new books by Columbia faculty: &lt;strong&gt;Ofer Dynes&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;rsquo;s &lt;em&gt;The Fiction of the State: The Partitions of Poland and the Beginning of Modern Jewish Literature&lt;/em&gt; (Stanford University Press, 2026), &lt;strong&gt;Yana Skorobogatov&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;rsquo;s &lt;em&gt;Fate in My Hands: The Death Penalty in the Soviet Union&lt;/em&gt; (University of Chicago Press, 2026), and &lt;strong&gt;Mark Lipovetsky&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;rsquo;s &lt;em&gt;The Soviet Trickster: Cynicism and Its Subversions&lt;/em&gt; (Cornell University Press, 2026). Moderated by &lt;strong&gt;Valentina Izmirlieva&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Each author will offer a brief overview of their work followed by a short Q&amp;amp;A period with the audience.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;About the Books&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The Fiction of the State&lt;/em&gt; locates the origins of modern Jewish literature in the political upheavals that transformed Europe at the end of the eighteenth century: the partitions of Poland, the French Revolution, and the Napoleonic Wars. As Austria, Russia, and Prussia consolidated their rule over the former Polish lands, Jewish intellectuals turned to prose, poetry, and drama to engage in political debate and make sense of the new imperial order. Drawing on archival research and close readings of Hebrew and Yiddish texts, Dynes presents modern Jewish literature not simply as the product of an internal struggle between tradition and modernity, but as a field of negotiation among multiple local and imperial belongings.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Fate in My Hands&lt;/em&gt; offers a history of the death penalty in the Soviet Union after Stalin told through the experiences of those who endured it. Following the reinstatement of the death penalty for homicide in 1954, nearly forty thousand Soviet citizens were executed before the collapse of the USSR. Drawing on previously inaccessible criminal-court records, as well as the letters and personal objects left behind by the condemned, Skorobogatov recovers the voices of prisoners who appealed for forgiveness and of the families, friends, and officials drawn into their cases.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The Soviet Trickster&lt;/em&gt; examines some of the most enduring rogues, jesters, swindlers, and transgressors in Soviet and post-Soviet literature, film, visual art, and performance. Lipovetsky explores the trickster&amp;rsquo;s revolutionary messianism, dubious ethics, relationship to empire, place in underground culture, and role in Soviet gender politics. Moving across the political and cultural history of the twentieth century, the book shows how the trickster could embody the cynicism and contradictions of Soviet modernity while also opening spaces for play, creativity, freedom, and subversion.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Join us for a screening of Gazelle (2025), a powerful independent drama following Yakup, a Turkish music teacher forced into exile in New York after political unrest at home. Undocumented and separated from his wife and daughter, Yakup confronts an impossible moral dilemma as legal pathways to reunite his family stall - a story that lays bare the psychological toll of displacement and the human cost of broken immigration systems. Directed by Nadir Sarıbacak and Samy Pioneer in their feature debut, Gazelle premiered at the 2025 Vancouver International Film Festival and offers a timely, deeply personal lens on exile, identity, and the fight to keep a family together. The screening will be followed by a discussion connecting the film&amp;#39;s themes to the human rights and immigration issues at the heart of our community&amp;#39;s work.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;When: &lt;/strong&gt;Monday, October 19 | 6:00-8:30 PM&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Where: &lt;/strong&gt;JG 107&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Speakers:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Nadir Sarıbacak (Co-Writer / Co-Director / Lead Actor)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;B&amp;uuml;şra &amp;Ccedil;oduroğlu (Assistant Director)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Food will be served.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Please register below:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Join us for a conversation with influencers, government officials and academics about our findings.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The administration of New York&amp;#39;s social media savvy mayor, Zohran Mamdani, is working closely with influencers to reach New Yorkers with information about voting, vaccines, pre K, participatory budgeting and other topics. Who is working with these influencers and what messages and programs are they promoting? New research from Columbia University sheds light on these questions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Moderator: Emily Bell, Director | Tow Center for Digital Journalism&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Panelists:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Anya Schiffrin, Senior Lecturer | SIPA&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Diego Leon, Content Creator | Dandy in the Bronx&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Dr. Jose Vilson,&amp;nbsp; Executive Director | EduColor&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Sari Beth Rosenberg, Educator and Influencer | NYC Department of Education&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.eventbrite.com/e/nyc-influencers-and-the-future-of-civic-engagement-tickets-2000953950129?aff=oddtdtcreator" target="_blank"&gt;RSVP required. Registration closes&amp;nbsp;24 hours before event start.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In recognition of America at 250 years, singers &lt;strong&gt;Sarah Fleiss &lt;/strong&gt;and &lt;strong&gt;Morgan Mastrangelo &lt;/strong&gt;and their baroque ensemble, &lt;strong&gt;Split the Lark, &lt;/strong&gt;explore the musical spirit of the time of the American Revolution.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;From figures like Antonio Vivaldi and Giovanni Battista Pergolesi&amp;mdash;who cemented the Italianate baroque style&amp;mdash;to Georg Frideric Handel and Thomas Arne&amp;mdash;who adopted these sensibilities for English-speaking audiences in London&amp;mdash;Italian opera was at the height of its power in the late 1700s. This musical form marked a shift in taste: from monarchical sway to public control. The ensemble will also present lesser-known works by Christoph Willibald Gluck, Nicola Porpora, and Pietro Alessandro Guglielmi, whose &lt;em&gt;La quakera spiritosa &lt;/em&gt;(&amp;ldquo;The Spirited Quaker&amp;rdquo;) details a young American girl&amp;rsquo;s rebellion against her father.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr /&gt;
&lt;p&gt;ABOUT THE ORGANIZERS&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Split the Lark &lt;/strong&gt;is a Baroque ensemble led by singers &lt;strong&gt;Sarah Fleiss &lt;/strong&gt;and &lt;strong&gt;Morgan Mastrangelo&lt;/strong&gt;. Along with a rotating cast of instrumentalists, Split the Lark seeks to codify an aesthetic practice of musicking that is not limited to the conditions of the initial performance. Drawing from cumulative soundings of this repertoire throughout history, both historical and speculative, Split the Lark takes vocal inspiration from bravura singers of the 19th century, up to and including the early stars of the recording era. Baroque theorists and writers upheld the channeling of &lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;les affets&amp;rdquo; &lt;/em&gt;(the &amp;ldquo;affectations&amp;rdquo;) as the greatest aim in music. Split the Lark believes that theatrical declamation&amp;mdash;the ability to be heard clearly and without strain from all parts of a room&amp;mdash;is the most potent tool to spark affectation across ensemble and audience.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Register &lt;a href="https://italianacademy.columbia.edu/events/italian-opera-age-american-revolution-baroque-ensemble-split-lark" target="_blank"&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Registration does not guarantee a seat. Check-in for registrants begins 30 minutes before the event. (Any open spots are offered to the stand-by line&amp;nbsp;10 minutes before the event).&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
This event is in-person only; there will be no&amp;nbsp;video recording for publication.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr /&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Websites for learning more about the performers:&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://splitthelark.co/" target="_blank"&gt;splitthelark.co&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;a href="https://www.sarahfleiss.com/" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;www.sarahfleiss.com&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.morganmastrangelo.com/" target="_blank"&gt;www.morganmastrangelo.com&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Speaker: &lt;/strong&gt;Dr. &lt;a href="https://josselin-garnier.org/"&gt;Josselin Garnier&lt;/a&gt;, Ecole Polytechnique&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Title:&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;quot;From Wave Statistics to Imaging in Random Media&amp;quot;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Abstract: &lt;/strong&gt;Wave propagation through randomly heterogeneous media arises in optics, acoustics, and seismic imaging. Random scattering can strongly distort the coherent wave field, making conventional imaging methods unreliable, but the statistical properties of the scattered field can still contain useful information.&lt;br /&gt;
In this talk, I will discuss mathematical models for wave propagation in random media and their implications for imaging. In the paraxial regime, multiscale analysis leads to an It&amp;ocirc;-Schr&amp;ouml;dinger equation that makes it possible to characterize the moments and statistical stability of the wave field. A central question is whether the field develops Gaussian statistics after long propagation, a property that holds in some regimes but fails in others.&amp;nbsp;&lt;br /&gt;
I will illustrate how these statistical properties affect imaging through examples involving speckle correlations and ghost imaging. I will also discuss random waveguides, where strong non-Gaussian fluctuations highlight both the limitations of correlation-based imaging and the potential value of information contained in higher-order statistics.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Bio:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;Josselin Garnier has been a professor of applied mathematics in France since 2001. He held positions in Toulouse, at Universit&amp;eacute; Paris Diderot and &amp;Eacute;cole Normale Sup&amp;eacute;rieure in Paris, before joining &amp;Eacute;cole Polytechnique in 2016. His research spans several areas of applied mathematics and probability, with particular interests in wave propagation and imaging in random and complex media, uncertainty quantification, and the design and analysis of stochastic algorithms.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr /&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;In person attendance at this seminar is only open to Columbia University affiliates. External guests are welcome to attend remotely. Please contact &lt;a href="mailto:apam@columbia.edu?subject=Zoom%20link%20needed%20for%20AM%20Colloquium" target="_blank"&gt;apam@columbia.edu&lt;/a&gt; if you need the Zoom link for this seminar. &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Joseph Zeira&lt;/strong&gt; joins the Institute for Israel and Jewish Studies for a discussion of the long-term economic impact of conflict, conscription, and defense spending in Israel.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tuesday, October 20, at 6:00 PM | 617 Kent Hall&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This talk describes the costs of the Israeli-Arab conflict to Israel. These are direct costs, which are mainly military spending, and indirect costs, which are mainly forgone output. During the period of the wide conflict with the Arab countries, between 1948 and 1980, the direct costs of the conflict were very high, as it involved conventional wars. After the Peace with Egypt the conflict became narrow, between Israel and the Palestinians mainly. This involved fighting with militias, which has low direct costs. However, the indirect costs remained high and even increased. First, every eruption of the conflict with the Palestinians causes a recession. Second, conscription delays entry of young people to their work career and that is costly as it reduces their human capital for many years. The largest indirect cost is a result of the high risk of the conflict, which deters investment. As a result, the conflict reduces the relative size of physical capital in Israel. Hence, the reduction of this risk could increase output by more than 26 percent.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In contrast to these high costs, the economic benefits of the conflict are rather low.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Joseph Zeira&lt;/strong&gt; is professor emeritus of Economics in the Hebrew University of Jerusalem. During the years, he visited Harvard, Brown, Brandeis, the University of Crete, Columbia University and Northwestern University. Joseph Zeira is a macroeconomist, who specializes in a number of areas: technology and economic growth, income distribution, money and liquidity, and the economy of Israel. His book, &lt;em&gt;The Israeli Economy: A Story of Success and Costs&lt;/em&gt;, was published in 2021 by Princeton University Press.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Joseph Zeira has also been involved in a number of professional activities, as an advisor to the Minister of Finance in Israel, as a board member of a Pension Fund, and in the Aix Group, where Israelis, Palestinians, and Internationals study the economic aspects of a potential peace agreement between Israel and Palestine. In 2014, he was a member of a government committee for reducing poverty in Israel. In 2016-2020, he served as the president of the Israeli Economic Association. Since 2020 he is the Chair of the Magid Institute for External Education in the Hebrew University.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.eventbrite.com/e/the-economic-costs-of-the-israeli-arab-conflict-with-joseph-zeira-tickets-1989682481864?aff=oddtdtcreator" target="_blank"&gt;Click here to register for this event&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr /&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Supported by the generosity of the Kaye and Appel families.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

