&lt;p&gt;The&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://cancerdynamics.columbia.edu" target="_blank"&gt;Herbert and Florence Irving Institute for Cancer Dynamics&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;hosts special seminars from time to time. The presentations are open to the Columbia community (in person and online) and to researchers outside Columbia (via Zoom).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;On &lt;strong&gt;Wednesday, October 21 (2:00 PM ET)&lt;/strong&gt;, IICD welcomes Dr. Harry Kabodha, co-founder of Varosync. Seminar hosted by Dr. Simon Tavar&amp;eacute;. The seminar will take place in person in Schermerhorn Hall 603 (Morningside Heights campus). If you wish to attend the seminar remotely, please register using the following link: &lt;a href="https://columbiauniversity.zoom.us/meeting/register/TgHYou7aTVul40Sg12ZCVg" target="_blank"&gt;https://columbiauniversity.zoom.us/meeting/register/TgHYou7aTVul40Sg12ZCVg&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Title: &lt;/strong&gt;From Cell States to Patients: Building Cancer Models That Survive Clinical Translation&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Abstract: &lt;/strong&gt;Computational models can describe molecular structure, cellular state, spatial organization, and tumor evolution in remarkable detail. Strong results on molecular or cellular data do not always predict what will happen in patients because important biological and clinical information can disappear between the model and the patient. Cases involving responses linked to biomarkers, acquired resistance, and toxicity caused by treatment show where these gaps appear. They also show how leaked information and tests that do not reflect the clinical decision can make benchmark results look stronger than they are. The talk ends with a practical way to judge whether a model is making a claim its data can support, whether that claim survives new evidence, and what result would show that it is wrong.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Bio: &lt;/strong&gt;Harry Kabodha is co-founder and CEO of Varosync, an applied research lab focused on clinical translation and therapeutic risk. He earned his MS in Biomedical Engineering from Columbia University and conducted research at the Irving Institute for Cancer Dynamics in Bianca Dumitrascu&amp;rsquo;s lab, where he studied generative approaches for reconstructing spatial information from scRNA-seq data. His current work examines how molecular biology, therapeutic history, patient heterogeneity, and clinical evidence can support more defensible decisions before major clinical and capital commitments are made.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If you would like to meet one-on-one (possibly via Zoom) or attend the lunch or dinner with the speaker, please contact the event organizer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Claire Sathe, MD&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
Assistant Professor of Medicine at the Columbia University Medical Center&lt;br /&gt;
Department of Medicine, Hematology &amp;amp; Oncology&lt;br /&gt;
Program: &lt;a href="https://www.cancer.columbia.edu/research/programs/cancer-population-science" target="_blank"&gt;Cancer Population Science&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Title:&lt;/strong&gt; Food Insecurity in Cancer Care: From Recognition to Intervention&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description: &lt;/strong&gt;Food insecurity is increasingly recognized as a consequence of the financial toxicity of cancer care, affecting patients with cancer at rates well above the general population. In this talk, I will first review the growing body of evidence linking food insecurity to lower treatment adherence, delayed or forgone care, psychological distress, and increased mortality, drawing in part on screening data from our own oncology clinics. I will then describe the emerging landscape of interventions designed to address food insecurity in oncology, including our ongoing pilot study of food navigation and delivery, highlighting early evidence of feasibility and their potential to improve engagement in cancer care.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Julia E McGuinness, MD&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
Assistant Professor of Medicine at the Columbia University Medical Center&amp;ensp;&amp;ensp;&amp;ensp;&amp;ensp;&amp;ensp;&lt;br /&gt;
Department of Medicine, Hematology &amp;amp; Oncology&lt;br /&gt;
Program: &lt;a href="https://www.cancer.columbia.edu/research/programs/cancer-population-science" target="_blank"&gt;Cancer Population Science&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Title: &lt;/strong&gt;Mammography-based biomarkers to assess response to breast cancer therapies&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description: &lt;/strong&gt;Mammography-based biomarkers of breast cancer risk, such as mammographic density, could refine breast cancer risk assessment and assess response to endocrine therapy among women with early breast cancer or at high risk for breast cancer. Advances in breast radiomics now incorporate machine learning techniques, which can evaluate imaging features beyond those visible to the human eye, and could enhance assessment of breast cancer risk. In this talk, I will discuss our work investigating breast radiomics tools that could be applied to standard breast imaging, including digital breast tomosynthesis (or &amp;quot;3D&amp;quot; mammograms) to potentially assess response to endocrine therapy (and other systemic therapies) among women with early breast cancer and who are at high risk for breast cance&lt;br /&gt;
