&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Colette: Pure, Impure, and in Translation&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
Rachel Careau, in conversation with Elisabeth Ladenson&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Colette&amp;rsquo;s writing has long had a reputation for being difficult to translate. Rachel Careau, translator of &lt;em&gt;Ch&amp;eacute;ri&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;La Fin de Ch&amp;eacute;ri,&lt;/em&gt; recently completed the first new translation of &lt;em&gt;Le Pur et l&amp;rsquo;impur&lt;/em&gt; in nearly sixty years, and only the second since its definitive 1941 publication. Of all Colette&amp;rsquo;s works, &lt;em&gt;The Pure and the Impure&lt;/em&gt;&amp;mdash;a wide-ranging, uncategorizable first-person exploration of sex and sexualities, the book that she considered her best and most autobiographical&amp;mdash;is perhaps the most challenging. Why? Acclaimed translator Rachel Careau and Columbia French professor Elisabeth Ladenson will discuss &lt;em&gt;Le Pur et l&amp;rsquo;impur&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;Ch&amp;eacute;ri&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;La Fin de Ch&amp;eacute;ri &lt;/em&gt;as well as Colette&amp;rsquo;s writing in general and what makes it, and &lt;em&gt;Le Pur&lt;/em&gt; in particular, so difficult to translate.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rachel Careau&lt;/strong&gt; is the translator most recently of Colette&amp;rsquo;s &lt;em&gt;The Pure and the Impure&lt;/em&gt; (Norton, 2026). Her previous translations include Colette&amp;rsquo;s&lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;Ch&amp;eacute;ri&amp;rdquo; and &amp;ldquo;The End of Ch&amp;eacute;ri&amp;rdquo;&lt;/em&gt; (Norton, 2022)&amp;mdash;a &lt;em&gt;New York Times Book Review&lt;/em&gt; Editors&amp;rsquo; Choice&amp;mdash;and the Austrian French writer Roger Lewinter&amp;rsquo;s &lt;em&gt;Story of Love in Solitude&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;The Attraction of Things&lt;/em&gt; (both New Directions, 2016). A 2019 National Endowment for the Arts Literature Translation Fellow, she lives in Hudson, New York.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Elisabeth Ladenson &lt;/strong&gt;is Professor of French and Comparative Literature at Columbia University. Her book &lt;em&gt;Proust&amp;#39;s Lesbianism&lt;/em&gt; (Cornell UP, 1999) has been translated into French as &lt;em&gt;Proust lesbien&lt;/em&gt; and into Spanish as &lt;em&gt;Lesbianismo en Proust&lt;/em&gt;.&amp;nbsp; Her book &lt;em&gt;Dirt for Art&amp;#39;s Sake: Books on Trial from Madame Bovary to Lolita &lt;/em&gt;was published by Cornell in 2007.&amp;nbsp; She is working on a book called &amp;quot;Proust in the Margins: Jew, Homosexual, Snob.&amp;quot;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Wednesday, Oct 21 | 6:10 PM |&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;Scheps Library, Room 457 Schermerhorn Extension&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Omar Dewachi (Rutgers University)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Join the Middle East Institute and the Center for the Study of Muslim Societies for a lecture with&amp;nbsp;Omar Dewachi about&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;Death of the Clinic: An Archaeology of Medical Undoing.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;em&gt;Death of the Clinic&amp;nbsp;&lt;/em&gt;traces the transformations of medicine across three decades of war, sanctions, occupation, and displacement in Iraq and the wider Middle East. Beginning in Baghdad&amp;rsquo;s Medical City during the sanctions of the 1990s, this talk follows wounds as they move through hospitals, military evacuation systems, laboratories, households, and across regional borders in search of care. Drawing on his experiences as a physician in Iraq and later anthropological research with Iraqi patients and clinicians across the region, Dewachi examines how the erosion of medical infrastructures reshapes not only where and how care takes place, but also the (micro)biological course of injury. The emergence of multidrug-resistant Acinetobacter baumannii during the Iraq War provides one window onto what he calls &amp;quot;war biology&amp;quot;: the entanglement of violence, therapeutic intervention, microbial adaptation, and survival over time. This talk asks what becomes of medicine when the clinic can no longer guarantee the continuity of care.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Omar Dewachi is a physician, medical anthropologist, and Associate Professor of Anthropology at Rutgers University, where he serves as Chair of the Department of Anthropology. His research and writing explore the social and political histories of medicine in Iraq and the wider Middle East, and the ways war, sanctions, displacement, and political upheaval reshape institutions, everyday life, and practices of care. He is the author of Ungovernable Life: Mandatory Medicine and Statecraft in Iraq (Stanford University Press, 2017), which examines the intertwined histories of medicine, state formation, and political violence in twentieth-century Iraq. His current book, Death of the Clinic, examines what remains of medicine amid the long aftermath of the 2003 invasion of Iraq and the wars and displacements that have reshaped the region over the past two decades.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For more information, please visit &lt;a href="https://www.mei.columbia.edu/mei-event/lecture-death-of-the-clinic-omar-dewachi"&gt;our website.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What does baseball have to do with religion? More than you&amp;rsquo;d think. &lt;em&gt;Baseball: Beyond Belief &lt;/em&gt;explores the profound, sometimes unlikely relationship between America&amp;rsquo;s pastime and the world&amp;rsquo;s great faith traditions, through the eyes of players, coaches, chaplains, rabbis, imams, and priests who have sat together in the same dugout.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Directed by Emmy Award&amp;ndash;winning filmmaker John Scheinfeld (&lt;em&gt;Dick Van Dyke, PBS American Masters&lt;/em&gt;), the film weaves together MLB archival footage, intimate interviews with Joe Torre and Curtis Granderson, and the voices of faith leaders across traditions. The result is a film about far more than baseball, it&amp;rsquo;s about how ritual, community, hope, and grace show up in the least expected places.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Join us for a screening of this film followed by a discussion with John Sexton, whose book,&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;Baseball as a Road to God:&amp;nbsp;Seeing Beyond the Game,&lt;/em&gt;&amp;nbsp;the film is based on.&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://its.law.nyu.edu/facultyprofiles/index.cfm?fuseaction=profile.biography&amp;amp;personid=20281" target="_blank"&gt;John Sexton&lt;/a&gt; served as president of New York University from 2001 through 2015.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
