&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&amp;quot;Beyond the Single Scan: From Generative Imaging and Foundation Models to Patient-Specific Digital Twins&amp;quot;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Gagandeep Singh, MD&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
Assistant Professor of Radiology&lt;br /&gt;
Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons&lt;br /&gt;
Columbia University Irving Medical Center&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Imaging AI in neuro-oncology is shifting from single prediction models toward connected systems that follow a patient over time. This seminar treats three technologies as complementary components of that evolution. Generative models fill in missing information, such as MRI sequences that were not acquired or are non-diagnostic. Foundation models learn reusable representations that can be applied across tasks and institutions. Digital twins aim to forecast how an individual patient&amp;#39;s disease may evolve over time. Using glioma as the test case, Dr. Singh will cover conditional MRI synthesis, multi-institutional foundation-model pretraining, imaging-based prediction of molecular markers, and longitudinal tumor modeling. The talk focuses on what it takes to build clinically relevant systems that can function within real-world clinical workflows and support patient care through rigorous validation and clinician oversight.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This research seminar is hosted by the Center for Innovation in Imaging Biomarkers and Integrated Diagnostics (CIMBID) and the Department of Biomedical Informatics.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
